# TypeScript Style

Biome (`bun run lint`) enforces formatting and its recommended rules. These conventions go beyond it.

## One keyed object parameter

Every public service, SDK, hook, and route helper takes a single object parameter, so call sites name their arguments and new options don't reorder anything.

```typescript
// Avoid
createProject(tenantId, "Acme", true);

// Prefer
createProject({ tenantId, name: "Acme", seed: true });
```

This matches the public surface: `forTenant({ tenantId }).forUser({ externalUserId })`, `useRuns({ projectId, workflowName })`, `findNodeAtPosition({ nodes, position })`. A private helper whose single argument is obvious (`slugify(text)`) may stay positional. Step functions always take one destructured object (see `AGENTS.md`).

## No `any`

Use `unknown` for untrusted or unknown values and narrow it with a Zod schema, a type guard, or `typeof`/`in` checks.

```typescript
function readOrder(input: unknown): Order {
  return OrderSchema.parse(input);
}
```

## No `as` casts

Let inference, generics, `satisfies`, and narrowing produce the type. `as const` is fine. Test fixtures may cast when building a partial object is the point of the test. When a library boundary genuinely erases a type (for example React Flow's `NodeProps.data`), keep the one cast at that boundary instead of spreading it. Never cast Kysely rows; use the generated `DB` types (see `project-model.mdc`).

## Few `let`s and little mutable state

Prefer `const` with expressions (ternaries, `map`, `filter`, early returns) over a `let` assigned in branches. Keep mutable state local to the function that owns it.
